From 418751ffdfbb2b6efca32301d1a0568d2e92f066 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 05:52:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 73/79] Make Google sign-in and sync unreachable

Patch 0010 removed the sign-in row, the Google services row and their section
header from the main settings list, and five other patches removed the promos,
the first run screen and the toolbar avatar. The pages themselves were never
taken away. A Pixel Fold showed the Google services page with an "Allow Chrome
sign-in" toggle on it, reached from a settings screen whose list offers no way
in.

Five routes were open. The fragment registry mapped /account and
/googleServices, so a deep link resolved. The settings search index still
advertised GoogleServicesSettings, PersonalizeGoogleServicesSettings and
ManageSyncSettings, and the search box sits at the top of the very screen the
rows were removed from. SettingsNavigationImpl returned the fragment classes for
three enum values. Privacy settings carried a footer whose links opened two of
the pages by class rather than through that enum, so guarding the enum alone
would have missed it; that footer only explains what Google does with sync and
account data, so it goes rather than being relinked to nowhere.

The fifth was the one that kept the page on screen. MainSettings removed the
rows at runtime, but main_preferences.xml still declared them, and on a tablet
two-pane layout the opening detail pane is resolved from that declaration. The
result was a Google services page opening by itself beside a list with no row
for it. The rows are gone from the declaration now, and the code that wired them
up is guarded, since findPreference returns null once they are not declared.

The subsystem is left in place. components/sync, components/signin and
chrome/browser/signin come to 773 files with no build flag to switch them off,
and bookmarks, passwords and history all have sync-aware paths that would break.
Nothing reachable, nothing advertised, rather than nothing compiled.

This also settles the forty-seven strings that name Chrome in a sign-in or sync
context. They describe pages a user can no longer open, so they need no
renaming.
